Glide64mk2: OverscanHorizontal and OverscanVertical, the pixels of the picture
that are cropped.

Video-Glide64mk2 gets two options, OverscanHorizontal (the pixels cropped on the
left and on the right) and OverscanVertical (on the top and on the bottom): 0 to
20 pixels (values out of that range are brought into it), of the N64's picture,
whose width is the one the game sets (320 or 640 usually). What is left of the
picture is scaled to fill its place on the screen, so the junk of the edges,
which a real TV hid, is not seen. The crop is not applied to the original size
(aspect 3).

It is done when the frame is presented, in the framebuffer object that
RenderHeight (0001) added: the picture's rectangle, less the crop, is blitted to
the picture's place in the window, and the window around it (the bars of a 4:3
picture on a 16:9 screen) is cleared. An overscan makes the plugin use the
framebuffer object even when RenderHeight is 0, so anti-aliasing is off then, as
it is with RenderHeight. OpenGL ES builds ignore it.

diff --git a/src/Glide64/Main.cpp b/src/Glide64/Main.cpp
